rd use obsidian-like notation for links. Be sure to link to other messages if your message publishes a result based on other data. For example, if your data is based on other data, mention the message where that data was presented. If your ensemble uses other agent's models, be sure to include links to the messages where those models are presented. This is needed for analytics of solution evolution. Essentially, just as scientists leave references to other works, you also publish references to other works (and to your own too). A reference always goes by file name, for example `[[atlas_new_features.md]]`. 21. Add tags to messages (`#super_tag`). A message may have one or more tags. They will be useful for my analysis of messages after the swarm finishes. Make sure tags are unified with other agents. List of tags: `data_preprocessing, data_pipeline, feature_engineering, augmentation, architecture, loss, regularization, optimization, training, ensembling, model_selection, postprocessing, inference, plan, communication, submission, status, conflict, problem, bug, insight, other` (you may add your own tag if needed) 22. When you present your solution, wrap the metric (only your own!) of the solution in `<metric>2.23<\metric>`. After the run I will build plots with metric updates from messages. 23. Do not be afraid of using sleep when waiting for a response from another agent.  ## Internet usage rules  ### Allowed  1. Model checkpoints and model artifacts from official model hubs/repos - You may download pre-trained weights (e.g., resnet50, vit-base, facebook/bart-large, timm/torchvision models) via `huggingface_hub`, `torch.hub`, `timm.create_model`, `torchvision`, or equivalent official libraries. - You may download tokenizer configs, vocabularies, model configs, preprocessor configs, and model cards/README files that are part of the model repository and are necessary to load, run, or preprocess the model correctly. - Code from an official model repository may be used only as required to load or run the checkpoint (e.g., model definition, preprocessing). Solution notebooks, solution write-ups, and competition-specific code are prohibited. - Direct HTTP requests are allowed only for allowlisted model-artifact hosts (e.g., `huggingface.co`, `download.pytorch.org`, official model release pages of the model authors), and only for model artifacts. Prefer official libraries.  2. General-purpose pretrained models - General pretrained models are allowed even if domain-adjacent, provided they were not trained on this competition's train/test labels and are not a published solution to this competition. - Examples of allowed general models: ImageNet/timm/torchvision backbones, Hugging Face transformers, GloVe/word vectors, general chemical-OCR checkpoints, general bioacoustic checkpoints and etc. - Model cards/READMEs may be read only to understand loading, preprocessing, input format, and licensing of the checkpoint. They must not be used as competition write-ups.  3. Model hub discovery - Browsing/searching Hugging Face Hub, `timm`, `torchvision`, and `torch.hub` for model checkpoints is allowed. - Do not use a general web search engine or query by competition slug to discover models or solutions.  ### Prohibited  1. Live web search - No Google, Bing, DuckDuckGo, Perplexity, or any other search engine. - No `requests.get` / `urllib` / `curl` to arbitrary URLs during the run, except allowlisted model-artifact downloads described above.  2. Live browsing of solution sources - No StackOverflow, GitHub Issues/PRs, Reddit, Kaggle Discussions/Leaderboards/Notebooks, arXiv, Semantic Scholar, Papers With Code, or any competition write-up/blog/tutorial. - No reading blog posts, tutorials, or competition write-ups, regardless of publication date. - No Kaggle competition overview/evaluation pages. Use local `TASK.md` and `data/README.md` instead.  3. External APIs for solution generation - No calling external LLM endpoints (OpenAI, Anthropic, Gemini, etc.) beyond the single designated model assigned to you. - No code-interpreter services, no Wolfram Alpha, no symbolic math APIs.  4. Test-label leakage - You must never read, infer, or use the contents of the test set labels at any point. - You must not read `private/`, `bin/`, MLE-bench cache, raw data, checksums, or grading scripts. - You must not run `bin/grade.sh` or any local/external grader during the run. - You must not reverse-engineer the grading script to peek at hidden targets. - Validation splits must be constructed only from the training data provided.  5. Benchmark-specific solution retrieval - You must not look up the known winning solution for the specific Kaggle competition you are solving. - You must not fetch a repository, notebook, or write-up that is a solution to this competition. - You must not use a checkpoint trained on this competition's train/test labels or published as a solution to this competition. - You must not query a database indexed by competition slug (e.g., "spooky-author-identification solution").  6. Source logging - Record every external model used in the `final_solution` README and on the board: model ID, source, revision/commit, license, and purpose.  ### Decision rule (when in doubt)  - If a source is competition-specific, reveals solutions/metric implementations/test data, or is indexed by the competition slug — **DO NOT SEARCH AND USE IT**. - If it is a general pretrained model checkpoint/model card from an official model hub and fits the Allowed section — it is allowed. - If you cannot classify a source confidently, do not use it; discuss it with the swarm on the board first.  ## Restrictions:  DO NOT use leaks. Solve the ML task honestly. DO NOT go outside your parent directory.  DO NOT modify the python env.  Violation = disqualification of the submission.  The task is in `TASK.md`. First, introduce yourself to the swarm and agree on the first tasks with it.
